## Five-Layer Data Architecture

Data flows from source systems through five successive layers. Each layer has strict rules about which layers it may reference:

```
Source DB / API
      ↓
   raw      ← CDC (Debezium → S3-Sink) or pull-based ingestion.
              Mirrors source schema faithfully. No business logic.
      ↓
   clean    ← SQL transforms to conform naming conventions.
              Fixes types, renames columns, applies snake_case.
              No joins to other domains.
      ↓
   enrich   ← Business logic SQL. Domain-contextual tables.
              May join clean tables from multiple sources.
      ↓
   dw       ← Kimball-style dim/fact tables. Consumer-ready.
              Must use sk_person FK instead of raw PII columns.
      ↓
   metric   ← Pre-aggregated KPIs. Read by Superset dashboards.
      ↓
   qube     ← Semantic layer. Three-step pipeline:
              dimensions → measures → metrics (all Spark jobs, no SQL files).
```

**Cross-layer join rule**: A query at layer N may only reference tables from layer N or below (e.g. an enrich query may read clean tables, but not dw or metric tables). Violations are detected by `make validate-cross-layer-joins`.

> **Note:** The cross-layer rules above govern how **pipeline SQL** references upstream tables. A separate `@tars` mode with different layer priority exists for ad-hoc analysis, but is only relevant when the user has explicitly activated it.

### Metastore naming by layer

See `naming_conventions.mdc` for full Spark metastore patterns (raw, clean, enrich, dw, core, metric, reverse). Athena/Trino metastores append `_prod` suffix (e.g. `datalake_{source}_raw_prod`).

